<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kham</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kham is a historical region of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, now divided mainly between the Tibet Autonomous Region and the Chinese provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, Qinghai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>. A land of deep river gorges along the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salween">Salween</a>, it was long split among local kingdoms and chieftaincies such as Derge. Its people, the Khampa, were known as traders and warriors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kham.jpg" alt="Kham" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lencer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tibetischer_Kulturraum_Karte.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kham is one of the three traditional Tibetan regions, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amdo">Amdo</a> in the northeast and Ü-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. Its terrain is a series of mountain ridges and gorges, the Hengduan Mountains, running from northwest to southeast, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, Yalong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salween">Salween</a> rivers flow through it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> have called it the Four Rivers and Six Ranges. The early trading route between central Tibet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> ran through Kham. Most residents speak Khams Tibetan, while at least a third speak Qiangic languages, a family of twelve interrelated languages not closely related to it. The Khampas were governed locally by chieftains and monasteries and are reputed warriors, known for marksmanship and horsemanship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Troops of the Tibetan Empire's Yarlung dynasty were sent east in the 7th century to establish Kham as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>'s eastern frontier, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>, who reigned from 629 to 649, built twelve border-taming temples there. After the Tibetan Empire broke into independent kingdoms in the mid-9th century, Kham was never controlled by a single king but remained a patchwork of kingdoms, tribes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, with Buddhist monasteries alongside secular rulers. Its main independent polities included Chakla, Degé, Lingtsang, Nangchen and Lhatok. In 1639 Güshri Khan, a supporter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, invaded with Mongolian troops and defeated the King of Beri.</p><p>After the Qing army defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a>, the frontier moved in 1725 or 1726 to the Dri River, the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, and the chieftains east of it received the title of tusi. From 1837 the chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonpo-namgyal">Gompo Namgyal</a> of Nyarong made himself the leading power in the region, until a Tibetan army defeated and killed him by 1865. Between 1904 and 1911 the Qing commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-erfeng">Zhao Erfeng</a>, nicknamed the Butcher of Kham, executed monks, destroyed monasteries and replaced local leaders with Chinese magistrates. In 1932 an agreement between the warlord Liu Wenhui and Tibetan forces divided Kham into an eastern part under Chinese administration and a western part under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, and an eastern area became Xikang Province in 1939.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1950 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> invaded western Kham, which became the Qamdo Territory and was merged into the Tibet Autonomous Region in 1965; Xikang was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> in 1955. Kham now includes 50 counties: 25 in the eastern Tibet Autonomous Region, 16 in Sichuan, six in Qinghai and three in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>. The border between Sichuan and the Tibet Autonomous Region remains at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kham” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In mainland Southeast Asia Peter Masefield has studied Indochinese or “<b>Kham</b>” Pali, long thought a degraded form but perhaps a consistent dialect, its changes caused by letter combinations hard to write in local scripts.</blockquote><a href="/language/pali">Pali · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>He moved to the Kokonor region, defeated the Khalkha prince Choghtu Khong Tayiji in 1637, subdued the king of Beri in <b>Kham</b> in 1640 and overthrew the Tsangpa dynasty in 1642.</blockquote><a href="/state/khoshut-khanate">Khoshut Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The family rose in the 15th century around the Sakya monastery of Lhundrup Teng, which became the royal seat, and gradually extended its rule over large parts of the upper Yangtze valley in <b>Kham</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-derge">Kingdom of Derge</a></li></ul></section>
